    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Correction authorization by publication record phase",
    "slot_value": "Pending records can usually be corrected by the original submitter or repository staff; approved records should be corrected by authorized staff or the original contributor if they have the proper role; records later found inaccurate should be directly intervened on by repository staff. Staff who can correct and moderate records themselves may edit normal bibliographic fields and moderate records still in draft, pending, or otherwise not yet finalized. Once a record is approved or published, changes to key fields or the attached full text should require a second review, especially if they affect the public record or reporting; unresolved conflicts should be flagged for another moderator or escalated to a higher authority rather than guessed. For duplicate/mismatch review, the moderator should compare the title, authors, DOI, year, venue, and any attached full text side by side, along with whichever fields the system thinks are uncertain. If the DOI matches exactly, that is a strong signal to treat it as the same publication, but the moderator should still confirm before merging. If the evidence is weak or conflicting, it should stay unresolved and be routed to a more experienced reviewer or escalated for manual checking. When duplicate records have weak or conflicting evidence, low-confidence matches with no critical impact can stay in a review queue for later; high-priority records, records with conflicting identifiers, or cases where there is a risk of merging the wrong publication or author should be escalated immediately to a more experienced reviewer. It should not automatically overwrite the record when new metadata appears; instead it should mark the record as having new evidence or a pending update, keep the migration flag until someone reviews it, and let a moderator decide whether to accept the correction to preserve the audit trail. Missing mandatory fields should reject the record from the main import and keep it in an error report or staging area for fix-and-reimport. Duplicates should not create a second record blindly; they should either link to the existing record or be flagged for review depending on how strong the match is. Ambiguous values may be imported as uncertain only if they can be clearly marked; otherwise they should remain in a manual review queue. Duplicate records affecting already published, widely cited, or reporting-linked items should be escalated sooner, especially when conflicting identifiers point to different authors or institutions. Simple uncertainty in a new or low-impact record can stay in the normal review queue if the reviewer can leave it untouched and move on. Only cases with clear publication-risk, such as a possible merge that could corrupt the official bibliographic record, should go to the more experienced reviewer immediately. For items quarantined during import because of mixed encodings or embedded attachments, staff should correct the record and re-import it, and extract an embedded attachment separately when feasible and link it back to the record; if it cannot be safely mapped or fixed, leaving the item out must be an explicit decision rather than an automatic drop.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

