    "slot_value": "Researchers or delegated staff can enter or update records; only authorized library or repository staff can approve and finalize them. Until approval, records are drafts or pending versions and not authoritative; after approval, the approved version becomes the one everyone relies on. For pending records, the submitter or repository staff can usually propose changes more freely; after approval, corrections should come from authorized staff or the original contributor if they have the proper role; if a record is later found to be inaccurate, repository staff can intervene directly as a data quality issue. Staff should be able to correct metadata, merge duplicate author or institution names, approve or reject pending records, run or re-run bulk imports, and fix common issues like missing fields, wrong classifications, or attaching the right full text, as long as it stays within their role. Technical support only needs to step in for system errors, failed integrations, import format problems staff cannot interpret, or workflow blocks outside the normal publication process. The system should prefill fields from the document, let a staff member review and correct them before finalization, clearly mark uncertain or ambiguous extraction so the reviewer knows what needs attention, and when there is a conflict with an existing record it should compare the records side by side and flag a possible duplicate or mismatch, with the final decision staying with the moderator. When users are searching, they should be able to refine the search and browse related records, and if that still fails they should have a fallback lookup path such as alternate author names, institution variants, or identifiers. Access or record status should limit what is shown if the publication is restricted or still under review, but the system should still indicate that a record exists when the user is allowed to know that.",
